      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i guess it's just trying to be helpful. it just feels so... chaotic? like the library knows something is wrong but it's just letting it slide and poking you about it instead of just stopping the process. do you think there's a way to just disable these warnings entirely if i'm sure my code is fine?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If you're confident that your code is correct and just want to avoid these warnings, you can indeed disable them. By default, most warnings in Python are category-based, meaning you can control them separately.\n\nIn pandas, you can use the `warnings` module to filter out these warnings. You can use the following code to disable the `SettingWithCopyWarning`:\n\n```python\nimport warnings\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=FutureWarning)\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=UserWarning)\n```\n\nAlternatively, you can also use `.cache()` on the DataFrame slice when working with it to ensure it's a copy:\n\n```python\ndf.loc[:, 'new_column'] = df.loc[:, 'old_column').copy()\n```",
      "source": "target",
